# Symbols in the grammar are case-sensitive.
# 
# This grammar uses a convention that
#    - terminals are usually lowercase  (president)
#    - preterminals are capitalized     (Noun)
#    - other nonterminals are all-caps  (NP)
# 
# This convention just makes grammars more readable to humans.  Thus:
#
#    - When *you* are writing grammars in questions 3 and 6, you should 
#      follow this convention unless you have a good reason not to.  
#
#    - But your *program* should still work with grammars that don't
#      follow this convention.  So how can your program reliably tell
#      the difference between terminal and nonterminal symbols?  If
#      there is at least one rule for rewriting a symbol, then that
#      symbol is a nonterminal and should be rewritten.
#######################

# Rules for creating full sentences.

1	ROOT	S .
1	ROOT	S !
1	ROOT	is it true that S ?     # mixing terminals and nonterminals is ok.
1   ROOT    that S perplexed NP .
1   ROOT    it perplexed NP that S .

# The basic grammar rules.  Here's what the abbreviations stand for:
#    S  = sentence
#    NP = noun phrase
#    VP = verb phrase
#    VP_PRE = verb phrase with present tense
#    VP_PAST = verb phrase with past tense
#    VP_FUTR = verb phrase with future tense
#    Verb_plu = verbs that are in plural form

#    PP = prepositional phrase
#    PN = person's name
#    PLNP = NP with plural form
#    Det = determiner (sometimes called "article")
#    Prep = preposition
#    Adj = adjective

#    Verb_trans_pre = transitive verb with present tense 
#    Verb_trans_past = transitive verb with past tense 
#    Verb_trans_futr = transitive verb with future tense 
#    Verb_intrans_pre = intransitive verb with present tense 
#    Verb_intrans_past = intransitive verb with past tense 
#    Verb_intrans_futr = intransitive verb with future tense 

#    Conj = conjunction
#    Verb_trans_clause = verb that can be followed by a that clause
#    Adv = adverb

2	S	NP VP
1   S   PLNP Verb_plu NP
1   S   NP Verb_trans_clause that S

1   VP VP_PRE
1   VP VP_PAST
1   VP VP_FUTR

1   VP_PRE Verb_trans_pre NP
1   VP_PRE Verb_intrans_pre
1   VP_PRE Verb_intrans_pre PP
1   VP_PAST Verb_trans_past NP
1   VP_PAST Verb_intrans_past
1   VP_PAST Verb_intrans_past PP
1   VP_FUTR Verb_trans_futr NP
1   VP_FUTR Verb_intrans_futr 
1   VP_FUTR Verb_intrans_futr PP 

2   Verb_trans_pre Verb_trans_pre Conj Verb_trans_pre
2   Verb_trans_past Verb_trans_past Conj Verb_trans_past
2   Verb_trans_futr Verb_trans_futr Conj Verb_trans_futr
2   Verb_intrans_futr Verb_intrans_futr Conj Verb_intrans_futr
2   Verb_intrans_past Verb_intrans_past Conj Verb_intrans_past
2   Verb_intrans_pre Verb_intrans_pre Conj Verb_intrans_pre

1   PLNP    NP Conj NP
1   PLNP    the Noun_com

2	NP	Det Noun
2	NP	Det Adj Noun
2	NP	Det Adv Adj Noun
1	NP	NP PP
1   NP  PN
1   PP  Prep NP
1  Adv  Adv Adv
1  Adj  Adj Adj

# Vocabulary.  Your program can see that "ate" is a terminal
# symbol because there exists no rule for rewriting it.
# Any symbol that can rewrite as a terminal (or a string of 
# terminals, like "chief of staff") is called a "preterminal."  Notice 
# that a preterminal is a special kind of nonterminal.

1   Verb_plu    choose
1   Verb_plu    eat
1   Verb_plu    want
1   Verb_plu    kiss
1   Verb_plu    understand
1   Verb_plu    pickle

1   Verb_trans_clause   thought

1   Verb_trans_pre eats
1   Verb_trans_pre has eaten
1   Verb_trans_pre is eating
1   Verb_trans_pre has been eating
1   Verb_trans_pre chooses

1   Verb_trans_past ate
1   Verb_trans_past had eaten
1   Verb_trans_past was eating
1   Verb_trans_past had been eating
2	Verb_trans_past	wanted
2	Verb_trans_past	kissed
2	Verb_trans_past	understood
2	Verb_trans_past	pickled
2	Verb_trans_past	chose

1   Verb_trans_futr will eat
1   Verb_trans_futr will have eaten
1   Verb_trans_futr will be eating
1   Verb_trans_futr will have been eating
1   Verb_trans_futr will choose

1   Verb_intrans_pre sighs
1   Verb_intrans_pre has sighed
1   Verb_intrans_pre is sighing
1   Verb_intrans_pre has been sighing
1   Verb_intrans_pre works
1   Verb_intrans_pre has worked
1   Verb_intrans_pre is working
1   Verb_intrans_pre has been working

1   Verb_intrans_past sighed
1   Verb_intrans_past had sighed
1   Verb_intrans_past was sighing
1   Verb_intrans_past had been sighing
1   Verb_intrans_past worked
1   Verb_intrans_past had worked
1   Verb_intrans_past was working
1   Verb_intrans_past had been working

1   Verb_intrans_futr will sigh 
1   Verb_intrans_futr will have sighed
1   Verb_intrans_futr will be sighing
1   Verb_intrans_futr will have been sighing
1   Verb_intrans_futr will work
1   Verb_intrans_futr will be working
1   Verb_intrans_futr will have worked
1   Verb_intrans_futr will have been working

1	Det	the
1	Det	a
1	Det	every

1	Noun	president
1	Noun	sandwich
1	Noun	pickle
1	Noun	chief of staff
1	Noun	floor
1   Noun    proposal
1   Noun    desk

1   Noun_com    citizens

1   PN  Sally

1	Adj	fine
1	Adj	delicious
1	Adj	perplexed
1	Adj	pickled

3	Prep	with
1	Prep	on
1	Prep	under
0.5	Prep	in

1   Conj    and

3   Adv     very